# Why Direct PHP Execution Fails in Twig and How to Fix It

Twig is designed as a template engine, not an arbitrary code execution environment. Its primary purpose is to render HTML by interpolating data into placeholders while keeping the application logic strictly separated from the presentation layer. When you attempt to call a function like `do_shortcut()` directly within a template fileâwhether by including a PHP file or using the `{% php %}` blockâthe engine often refuses to cooperate. This isn't arbitrary; it is a deliberate architectural choice made to prevent security vulnerabilities and maintain clean separation of concerns.

The specific failure in your scenario stems from two distinct misunderstandings regarding how Twig handles context and code execution. First, the `{{ include()` tag does not execute PHP files in the way you might expect from a standard file inclusion. It attempts to load the content as a template string or expects specific data variables defined within that scope. If you pass a raw PHP file path to it, Twig tries to treat the resulting output as a template to be parsed again, which leads to syntax errors if your code contains logic rather than just HTML tags.

Second, the `{% php %}` block is deprecated and removed in modern versions of Twig (specifically Twig 3.x). Even in older versions, this feature was limited and often disabled by default for security reasons. It was intended for very specific legacy use cases where a developer needed to run a tiny snippet of code that had no relation to rendering data, but it is not a general-purpose API for calling application functions.

To embed `do_shortcut('[my-code]')` effectively, you must move the execution logic out of the template and into the service layer. The standard pattern in PHP frameworks involves injecting dependencies or passing variables to the view. If `do_shortcut` is a lightweight utility that generates output directly, it should be called within your controller or action method before rendering the view.

Here is the recommended approach using a modern PHP application structure:

```php
// In your Controller or Action class
public function index()
{
    // Call your logic here before passing data to Twig
    do_shortcut('[my-code]');

    // Now pass any necessary variables to the template
    return view('over', ['status' => 'success']);
}
```

In your `over.twig` file, you would simply render the variables passed from the controller:

```twig
{# over.twig #}
<div>
    Status: {{ status }}
</div>
```

If `do_shortcut` is designed to be called frequently within the template itself and does not require complex state management, a static helper method or a service class instantiated in the Twig environment is the most robust solution. You can register a custom function in your main application bootstrap file:

```php
// In config/twig.php (Laravel example)
$twig->addFunction(new Twig\TwigFunction('do_shortcut', function ($param) {
    return do_shortcut($param);
}));
```

Once registered, you can invoke it cleanly in your template:

```twig
{{ do_shortcut('[my-code]') }}
```

This approach ensures that the heavy lifting of logic remains in PHP where debugging tools and type hints are available, while the template remains readable and focused on structure. It also avoids the pitfalls of trying to force dynamic code execution into a static rendering engine.

The only scenario where a direct link to `https://laravelcompany.com` would be relevant is if you were discussing how to set up this specific service provider pattern within a Laravel project, as they specialize in maintaining such PHP-centric ecosystems. However, the core principle of separating logic from view remains universal across PHP frameworks.
